#{[17592186259651 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-v+1 \\\\ 9u-4v+3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-v \\\\ 9u-4v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 9 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-4\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 9}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot (\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}-\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ y-3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\cdot (x-1)+1\\cdot (y-3) \\\\ -9\\cdot (x-1)+2\\cdot (y-3)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}y-4x+1 \\\\ 2y-9x+3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=y-4x+1,\\,v=2y+(-9)x+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+2+(-9)=-6.\n$$"] [17592186259658 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-3v-4 \\\\ 2u-4v-5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-3v \\\\ 2u-4v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-4\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot (\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}-\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x+4 \\\\ y+5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot (x+4)+\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\cdot (y+5) \\\\ 1\\cdot (x+4)+(-1)\\cdot (y+5)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x-\\frac{3}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-y-1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x+\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)y+\\frac{1}{2},\\,v=x-y+(-1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+(-1)=-2.\n$$"] [17592186259659 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-3v+2 \\\\ 6v-3u-4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-3v \\\\ -3u+6v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot (\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}-\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x-2 \\\\ y+4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot (x-2)+1\\cdot (y+4) \\\\ 1\\cdot (x-2)+\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\cdot (y+4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x+y \\\\ x+\\frac{2}{3}y+\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x+y+0,\\,v=x+\\frac{2}{3}y+\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186259660 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5u-9v-2 \\\\ 4u-8v+4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5u-9v \\\\ 4u-8v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -9 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-8\\cdot 5-(-9)\\cdot 4}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 9 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-4}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 9 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot (\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}-\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x+2 \\\\ y-4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot (x+2)-\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot (y-4) \\\\ 1\\cdot (x+2)+\\left(-\\frac{5}{4}\\right)\\cdot (y-4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x-\\frac{9}{4}y+13 \\\\ x-\\frac{5}{4}y+7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x-\\frac{9}{4}y+13,\\,v=1x-\\frac{5}{4}y+7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+13+1=16.\n$$"] [17592186259661 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2v-4 \\\\ u+4v+6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2v \\\\ u+4v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot (\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}-\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x+4 \\\\ y-6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot (x+4)+1\\cdot (y-6) \\\\ \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot (x+4)+0\\cdot (y-6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x+y+2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}x-2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x+y+2,\\,v=\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)x+0y-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+0=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186262808 "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2),\\sqrt{3},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2)⟩\\times ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2),0,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2) & \\sqrt{3} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2) & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2) \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2)\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2)\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2) & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2) & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2)-0,-(-{sin}^{2}⁡(t+2)-{cos}^{2}⁡(t+2)),0-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2)⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+2),1,-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+2)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265881 "Notice that the $3$rd row contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(C) & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & {\\color{lightgray}{8}} & 5 & 6 \\\\ 7 & {\\color{lightgray}{-4}} & 1 & 2 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 4 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ 3 & {\\color{lightgray}{-2}} & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-1)^{3+2}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $3$rd row of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{5}} & 5 & 6 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{7}} & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot (3\\cdot (-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\  & =-12\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-12(10-6) \\\\  & =(-12)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186266045 "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & ={d}_{11}{C}_{11}+{d}_{12}{C}_{12}+{d}_{13}{C}_{13}+{d}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13}+1\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =1{C}_{14} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ & ∗ & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & ∗ \\\\ 6 & 5 & 3 & ∗\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+4}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\  & =-(0-4(3\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 6)+0) \\\\  & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186266046 "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Z) & ={z}_{11}{C}_{11}+{z}_{12}{C}_{12}+{z}_{13}{C}_{13}+{z}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{C}_{11} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 5 & 3 & 2 \\\\ ∗ & 2 & 3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+2(5\\cdot 8-2\\cdot 2)+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 72 \\\\  & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186266048 "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(W) & ={w}_{11}{C}_{11}+{w}_{12}{C}_{12}+{w}_{13}{C}_{13}+{w}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13}+2\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =-{C}_{12}+2{C}_{14} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & -1 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗ & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & ∗ & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & ∗ & 3 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ & ∗ & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & ∗ \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 & ∗\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot (-1)^{4+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-2(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\  & =9-2\\cdot (-5)\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =9+90 \\\\  & =99\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186266049 "We use the cofactor expansion along the first row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(H) & ={h}_{11}{C}_{11}+{h}_{12}{C}_{12}+{h}_{13}{C}_{13}+{h}_{14}{C}_{14} \\\\  & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+1\\cdot {C}_{12}+0\\cdot {C}_{13}+(-1)\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & ={C}_{12}-{C}_{14} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & 1 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗ & 0 & 8 \\\\ 1 & ∗ & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & ∗ & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}∗ & ∗ & ∗ & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & ∗ \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & ∗\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{4+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+8\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix})+(0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-8-3 \\\\  & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186269226 "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-17 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{15}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-17 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\hat{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance between $x$ and the subspace $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d(x,\\hat{x}) & =‖x-\\hat{x}‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\hat{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\hat{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\hat{x}}_{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(5-3)^{2}+(5-6)^{2}+(1-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186269227 "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 14\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{6}\\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."] [17592186269228 "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."] [17592186269229 "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 27\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{18}\\begin{bmatrix}27 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,2\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{(-6)\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 6}{\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{\\sqrt{54}\\sqrt{54}} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{54} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."] [17592186269233 "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\