{3}(1))}\\,⊕\\,{3}^{({J}_{1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & \\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292564 "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({J}_{3}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(3))}^{4} & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{3}(2) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{1}(3)\\end{bmatrix})}^{4} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{({J}_{3}(2))}^{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {({J}_{1}(3))}^{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={({J}_{3}(2))}^{4}\\,⊕\\,{({J}_{1}(3))}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{3}(2),$ we have $n=3$ and $\\lambda =2.$ \\since $n=3,$ we need to compute the first and second derivatives of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(2)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(2) & {f}^{′}(2) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(2)}{2} \\\\ 0 & f(2) & {f}^{′}(2) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{4} & 4\\cdot {2}^{3} & \\frac{12\\cdot {2}^{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 & {2}^{4} & 4\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {2}^{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 32 & 24 \\\\ 0 & 16 & 32 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(3),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =1.$ since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(3)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(3)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}81\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({J}_{3}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(3))}^{4} & ={({J}_{3}(2))}^{4}\\,⊕\\,{({J}_{1}(3))}^{4} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 32 & 24 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 16 & 32 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 16 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 81\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186292876 "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, it must satisfy $T(kv)=kT(v).$ Noting that $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}=2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}) & =T(2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix})=2T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we're told that \n\n$$\nT(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295086 "Performing the operations on each corresponding entry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(B-C) & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3-1 & 5-2 \\\\ 0-6 & -4-3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4-2 & -2-3 \\\\ 7-(-6) & 1-(-7)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 13 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186295087 "Performing the operations on each corresponding entry, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X+(Y-Z) & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}+(\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix